      {
        "id": "p3-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "By factorisation, explain why $6!+4$ will be divisible by $4$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$6!+4=4(6\\times5\\times3\\times2\\times1+1)$, so it is divisible by $4$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}6!+4&=6\\times5\\times4\\times3\\times2\\times1+4\\\\6!+4&=4(6\\times5\\times3\\times2\\times1+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe expression in brackets is an integer, so $6!+4$ is divisible by $4$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By factorisation, explain why $7!+3$ will be divisible by $3$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$7!+3=3(7\\times6\\times5\\times4\\times2\\times1+1)$, so it is divisible by $3$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}7!+3&=7\\times6\\times5\\times4\\times3\\times2\\times1+3\\\\7!+3&=3(7\\times6\\times5\\times4\\times2\\times1+1).\\end{align*}$$\nThe expression in brackets is an integer, so $7!+3$ is divisible by $3$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p3-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Consider the sequence of $n-1$ consecutive natural numbers\n$$n!+2,\\ n!+3,\\ n!+4,\\ \\ldots,\\ n!+n.$$\nShow that none of these is a prime number. Note that this shows that there are sequences of consecutive composite numbers of arbitrary length.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For every integer $k$ with $2\\le k\\le n$, $n!+k=k\\left(\\frac{n!}{k}+1\\right)$ is composite.\n\nHere $k$ divides $n!$, and both factors are greater than $1$. Choosing $n=L+1$ gives $L$ consecutive composite numbers.",
          "worked": "Let $k$ be an integer with $2\\le k\\le n$. Since $k$ is a factor in the product $n!$, $\\frac{n!}{k}$ is a positive integer.\n$$\\begin{align*}n!+k&=k\\left(\\frac{n!}{k}+1\\right).\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors are greater than $1$, so $n!+k$ is composite. This applies to every term of the sequence. For any desired positive length $L$, choose $n=L+1$; the sequence then contains $L$ consecutive composite numbers.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 54
      }
    ]
  },
